"Surah ar-Rahman begins with Allah's name ar-Rahman. ar-Rahman means the One whose mercy and boundless favor surge forth in abundance. In this verse, it is emphasized that Allah is ar-Rahman. In the second verse that follows, it is stated that ar-Rahman taught the Qur'an. The verb 'ta'lim' denotes the teaching of the Qur'an; this teaching encompasses the entirety of teaching to Gabriel, to the Prophet, and to the ummah. Some scholars have stated that this verb means 'to make a sign'; according to this, the Qur'an was made a sign for those who take admonition and a miracle for prophethood.\n\nIn the third verse, it is stated that ar-Rahman created man. What is meant by man could be Adam, Muhammad (peace be upon him), or the human species; the most correct view is the human species. In the fourth verse, it is expressed that man was taught clear expression (bayan). Bayan means a person's clear expression of the feelings and thoughts within them to others. This is the blessing of speech and language; the acquisition of knowledge and the teaching of the Qur'an become possible through this. The teaching of the names of things to Adam was also by virtue of the knowledge of bayan.\n\nIn the fifth verse, it is stated that the sun and the moon move according to a precise calculation. This indicates that people should understand calculation well and be prepared for the Day of Reckoning. In the sixth verse, it is recounted that plants and trees prostrate, submitting to the will of Allah. In the seventh verse, it is expressed that the sky was raised and the balance (mizan—the law of equilibrium) was established. Mizan is the universal law of balance and justice; it is the foundation of gravity and measuring instruments such as scales, and it ensures that everything stands in accordance with its due right.\n\nIn the eighth verse, the reason for establishing the balance is so that injustice is not committed in weighing. In the ninth verse, it is commanded to maintain the weight with justice and not to make the balance deficient. In the tenth verse, it is stated that the earth was laid low for creatures, that is, the earth was made suitable for living beings. This is a manifestation of Allah's mercy.\n\nIn the eleventh verse, it is stated that there are fruits and date palms with spathes on the earth. In the twelfth verse, husked grains (such as wheat and barley) and fragrant plants (rayhan) are mentioned. Rayhan also means provision. In the thirteenth verse, the address 'Which of the favors of your Lord will you twain deny?' is directed to humans and jinn. This verse emphasizes that favors must not be denied; at each of its repetitions, it is recommended (mandub) to say, 'No, we do not deny any of Your favors, O our Lord; praise be to You.'\n\nIn the fourteenth verse, it is explained that man was created from sounding dry clay (salsal), and in the fifteenth verse, that the jinn (jann) were created from a smokeless flame of fire. The human and jinn species are mentioned separately. Marij fire has been interpreted as a smokeless pure flame or a fire that penetrates all things. This difference in creation reflects the characteristics of both types of beings.\n\nIn the seventeenth and eighteenth verses, it is emphasized that ar-Rahman is the Lord of the two easts and the two wests. This expression may refer to the rising and setting points of the sun and the moon, the summer and winter solstices, the spherical shape of the earth, or material and spiritual lights. In the nineteenth verse, it is related that the two seas were released and meet each other. These seas have been interpreted in various ways, such as sweet and salty, the Indian Ocean and the Mediterranean, or the heavenly sea and the earthly sea. The meeting of the two seas has also been understood as an allusion to the opening of the Suez Canal.\n\nThere is a barrier (barzakh) between the two seas. Barzakh means the boundary and separating barrier between two things. In geography, it is also applied to the land between two seas. Thanks to the barrier between them, these two seas do not mix with each other, and the water of one does not impair the characteristic of the other. This is an evidence of Allah's power.\n\nFrom these seas emerge pearls and coral. Pearls and coral are used both as ornaments and as blessings of commerce. In particular, large pearls and red coral are valuable.\n\nShips floating like mountains upon the sea are also among Allah's blessings. The expression 'jawari al-munsha'at' means ships that are constructed or whose sails are unfurled. Although these ships are made by human beings, in reality they exist through Allah's creation. Furthermore, this expression also alludes to celestial bodies flowing along by Allah's power.\n\nEverything on earth is mortal and perishing. Only the Face (Essence) of your Lord, Owner of Majesty and Honor, remains enduring. The word 'wajh' (face) is in the meaning of Allah's Essence. This attribute demonstrates both Allah's grandeur and His grace. The expression 'Dhu al-Jalali wa'l-Ikram' is an exclusive name belonging to Allah and is used as a great supplication.\n\nEveryone in the heavens and the earth asks of Allah. Every day He is upon a new affair. This affair is related either to majesty or to honor. At every moment, Allah creates, destroys, raises, or lowers whatever He wills. The expression 'every day upon a new affair' means either that He is engaged in a new work at every moment, or that He has two distinct affairs: this world and the Hereafter.\n\nO company of humans and jinn! When the Day of Reckoning arrives, Allah will direct His attention to you and settle your account. You are called 'thaqalan' because you are like heavy burdens borne on the earth. On that day, if you had the power to pass beyond the regions of the heavens and the earth, you would flee, but you can only pass through with permission from Allah. If you attempt to flee, a flame of fire and smoke like molten brass will be sent upon you, and you will neither be able to defend yourselves nor be saved.\n\nWhen the Resurrection takes place, the sky will split asunder and turn into a crimson rose like molten oil. The terror of that day cannot be described. On that day, there will be no need to interrogate the criminals about their sins, because they will be recognized by their marks; they will be seized by their forelocks and feet and cast into Hell. They will circulate between Hell and boiling water. Which of the favors of your Lord will you twain deny?\n\nOn the Day of Resurrection, the sins of the guilty will not be questioned, because they will be recognized by their faces. The sinful and the sinless will not be confused; everyone's state is determined. The terror of that day, separating the obedient from the disobedient and exacting retribution from the disbelievers, is also a blessing. When the sky splits asunder and becomes like a red rose, terrifying upheavals will occur.\n\nThe criminals will circle round between the fire of Hell and scalding hamim (boiling water). They will suffer the punishment for their disbelief and ingratitude. After the manifestations of majesty belonging to the Lord, Owner of Majesty and Honor, have been thus expounded, the manifestations of His honor are recounted.\n\nFor those who fear the station of their Lord, there are two gardens. The word 'maqam' (station) means Allah's sovereignty over all things or the standing of mankind before Him for reckoning on the Day of Resurrection. Fear is not merely a palpitation of the heart, but abstaining from disbelief and obeying with faith and gratitude. These two gardens may be physical and spiritual; their details are not known.\n\nBoth gardens possess diverse trees, fruits, and two flowing springs. Of every fruit there are pairs. They recline on couches lined with rich brocade, and the harvest of the fruits hangs within reach. Therein are maidens who restrain their glances solely to their spouses, whom neither human nor jinn has touched before them. They are like rubies and coral.\n\nBesides these two gardens, there are two other gardens; they are dark green, with two gushing springs, and all kinds of fruit, date palms, and pomegranates. Within them are women of noble character and lovely countenances, houris secluded in pavilions with their eyes turned toward their husbands. Neither human nor jinn has touched them before either. The people of Paradise will recline on green cushions and beautifully embroidered couches.\n\nThe reward of goodness is nothing but goodness. In the narrations concerning the occasion of revelation of this verse, it is reported that Abu Bakr, pondering the terror of the Day of Judgment, said, 'If only I were greenery,' and that during the time of Umar, when a young man died of his passionate love, Umar said, 'For you there are two gardens.' This demonstrates the importance of fearing Allah and acting with ihsan (excellence in worship and virtue).\n\nThe reward for the one who attains the blessing of Tawhid (the oneness of Allah) is Paradise. The expression 'qasiratu't-tarf' means beauties who direct their glances only toward their husbands, or who draw the gaze of the beholder to themselves. As for 'tamth,' it means virginity or not having been touched. Our Lord is the Lord of Generosity; how exalted is His name!\n\nIn the 62nd and 63rd verses, Allah Almighty informs that, besides the two gardens mentioned earlier, there are two more gardens on this side of them or beyond them. According to Ibn Zayd, the earlier gardens are for the muqarrabun (those brought near to Allah), whereas these subsequent ones are for the ashab al-yamin (the companions of the right), whose records of deeds are given in their right hands. Hasan al-Basri also stated that the former are the Companions, and the latter are the Successors (Tabi'un). According to both views, the second gardens are lower than the first in honor and rank. The former are described with more concise and mature attributes.\n\nIn the 64th and 65th verses, the color of these gardens is described as 'lush green.' The word 'mudhammatan' is derived from 'duhma,' which means the darkness of night and the color of a dark horse. Here, it denotes an intense, deep dark-green color. Ibn Abbas, Mujahid, and others interpreted this word as 'khadrawan,' that is, lush green. According to the narration of Abu Ayyub (may Allah be pleased with him), the Prophet (peace be upon him) also stated that this word means khadrawan.\n\nIn the 66th and 67th verses, it is stated that in these gardens there are 'two gushing springs.' 'Nadh' means the gushing of water; the form written with a dotted 'ha' indicates a more vigorous gushing. These gushing fountains are considered more artistic and beautiful than flowing springs.\n\nIn the 68th and 69th verses, it is described that in both gardens there are various kinds of fruits, date palms, and pomegranates. The nunation (tanwin) indicates that these fruits are of an unprecedentedly superior nature. The specific mention of date palms and pomegranates is because, in addition to being fruits, they possess benefits such as sustenance and medicine. The seedless pomegranate is also particularly noteworthy here.\n\nIn verses 70–75, the houris, who are the spouses of Paradise, are depicted. The word 'hur' means a beauty whose eye-whites are intensely white and whose pupils are intensely black. They are chaste maidens inside pavilions (tents), gazing only upon their husbands. In a hadith of the Prophet (peace be upon him), it is reported that these pavilions are made of a hollow pearl and their height is sixty miles. According to al-Razi, this expression shows that in Paradise items move toward the believers, and that they attain every blessing without fatigue.\n\nIn the 76th verse, it is described that the people of Paradise recline on green rafrafs and beautiful abqaris. 'Rafraf' carries various meanings such as fine fabric, a mattress, a spread, or green meadows. 'Abqari,' on the other hand, refers to embroidered and precious fabrics attributed to the settlement of Abqar, once located in China or Yemen. Although some scholars attributed abqari to the jinn, it actually denotes bedding and carpets of superior quality.\n\nThe commentators have expressed various views regarding the meanings of the words abqari and rafraf. For rafraf, meanings such as green fabric, mattress, bedsheet, spread, curtain, cushion, raised couch, and a garden of Paradise have been given. As for abqari, it has generally been used in the sense of beautiful and high-quality fabric; in one narration it was attributed to a region in China, while some interpreted it as a place where the jinn reside.\n\nRegarding the origin of the word abqari, al-Farra defined it as thick fabric, Mujahid as silk fabric, Qatadah as carpets and rugs, and Sa'id ibn Jubayr as antique furnishings. These narrations indicate that abqari is not attributed to a place, but is a noun in its own right. In conclusion, rafraf can be considered as the meadows of Paradise, and the abqaris as the fine garments of the people of Paradise.\n\nIn Surah ar-Rahman, the same verse is repeated thirty-one times. Eight of these occur after the subjects of creation and the worldly life and the Hereafter, seven after the warnings concerning Hell, and eight after the descriptions of the first two gardens. These numbers indicate that the gates of Hell are seven and the gates of Paradise are eight. Thus, whoever believes in and acts upon the first eight matters enters both gardens and is protected from Hell.\n\nAt the end of the surah, the name of your Lord is mentioned as tabaraka (blessed and exalted). The expression 'Dhu'l-jalali wa'l-ikram' (Possessor of Majesty and Generosity) emphasizes that Allah is the Possessor of greatness and bounty. The word tabaraka here signifies exaltedness, abundance of good, and blessing. Al-Alusi states that the attribution of this verb to the name of Allah is permissible, because mercy is sought through its honor.\n\nThe expression 'they recline' in the verse indicates that the people of Paradise are perpetually in tranquility and rest. According to al-Razi, while people in this world find themselves in varying states, in Paradise there is no fatigue or exertion. For this reason, in the verses, ittiqa (avoidance/caution) is mentioned sometimes earlier and sometimes later. In addition, words such as rafraf and abqari are meant to convey the indescribable beauty of the blessings of Paradise."